1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,225
This sock got rained on in June.

2
00:00:02,338 --> 00:00:03,950
I hung it up to dry that night.

3
00:00:04,062 --> 00:00:05,225
It's November now.

4
00:00:05,338 --> 00:00:06,338
Still damp.

5
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:09,625
The village that did this sits
in the hills of northeast India.

6
00:00:09,737 --> 00:00:11,175
Hi, I'm Wob.

7
00:00:11,288 --> 00:00:14,138
I visit odd places and
ask why anyone stays.

8
00:00:14,714 --> 00:00:18,176
The village is Mawsynram, in
the Khasi Hills of Meghalaya,

9
00:00:18,226 --> 00:00:20,614
on the edge of a plateau above Bangladesh.

10
00:00:20,726 --> 00:00:23,676
I went there in June to move
in, with every sock I own.

11
00:00:23,789 --> 00:00:26,252
I came home with this one, a small broom,

12
00:00:26,302 --> 00:00:28,264
and a bottle of water I paid for.

13
00:00:28,376 --> 00:00:31,689
In one of the rainiest places
on Earth, I paid for water.

14
00:00:31,802 --> 00:00:36,052
By the end of this trip, the broom and the
bottle explained more than the rain did.

15
00:00:36,164 --> 00:00:39,576
But first, I had some very
confident wrong ideas.

16
00:00:40,144 --> 00:00:43,169
I arrived in a taxi with
its wipers on full panic.

17
00:00:43,281 --> 00:00:44,931
My first theory was fame.

18
00:00:45,044 --> 00:00:47,394
A place this wet must live off being wet.

19
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:51,982
The World Meteorological Organization
lists Mawsynram as the record holder for

20
00:00:52,032 --> 00:00:54,106
average yearly rainfall in Asia.

21
00:00:54,219 --> 00:00:57,594
About eleven thousand eight hundred
and seventy-two millimetres,

22
00:00:57,657 --> 00:01:00,719
averaged from nineteen forty-one
to nineteen seventy-nine.

23
00:01:00,832 --> 00:01:03,469
That's about four hundred
and sixty-seven inches.

24
00:01:03,582 --> 00:01:07,419
Left to pile up, one year of it would
stand almost twelve metres deep.

25
00:01:07,532 --> 00:01:09,069
About thirty-nine feet.

26
00:01:09,181 --> 00:01:11,019
Taller than a three-storey house.

27
00:01:11,131 --> 00:01:13,169
Is it the wettest place
on the whole planet?

28
00:01:13,281 --> 00:01:14,781
Depending on who's measuring.

29
00:01:14,894 --> 00:01:19,156
Nearby Cherrapunji and two spots in
Colombia have numbers of their own.

30
00:01:19,269 --> 00:01:20,944
So I went looking for the fame.

31
00:01:21,057 --> 00:01:22,982
Mostly, I found more rain.

32
00:01:23,094 --> 00:01:25,519
Nobody moves house for
a weather statistic.

33
00:01:25,631 --> 00:01:28,569
My second theory was that
the rain keeps everyone in.

34
00:01:28,682 --> 00:01:30,732
So I asked a taxi to take me out.

35
00:01:30,844 --> 00:01:31,644
It did.

36
00:01:31,719 --> 00:01:34,781
Shillong is about sixty
kilometres away by road,

37
00:01:34,856 --> 00:01:36,169
under forty miles.

38
00:01:36,281 --> 00:01:38,994
Landslides block roads
around here sometimes,

39
00:01:39,044 --> 00:01:42,444
and in the wettest months the rain
can cut some villages off for weeks.

40
00:01:42,557 --> 00:01:44,719
But the road works, both ways.

41
00:01:44,831 --> 00:01:48,106
I rode out, had a dry cup
of tea, and rode back.

42
00:01:48,219 --> 00:01:49,544
Nobody was trapped.

43
00:01:49,656 --> 00:01:50,694
They could leave.

44
00:01:50,807 --> 00:01:51,657
They stay.

45
00:01:52,230 --> 00:01:56,742
On my second morning I went out with
an umbrella A good one The rain came in

46
00:01:56,792 --> 00:02:02,443
sideways The umbrella turned inside out
then outside in then left without me That's

47
00:02:02,493 --> 00:02:07,280
when I met the knup It's a rain shield woven
from bamboo and layered with dried palm

48
00:02:07,330 --> 00:02:12,268
leaves sometimes with a plastic sheet these
days It sits over your head and back and

49
00:02:12,318 --> 00:02:18,167
reaches down to your knees Basically a roof
you wear Women here make them I got one

50
00:02:18,217 --> 00:02:23,905
put it on and stood in a downpour dry hands
in my pockets like a very smug tortoise

51
00:02:24,017 --> 00:02:28,243
Then I saw what the free hands are for
People in knups were heading down the slopes

52
00:02:28,293 --> 00:02:34,243
below the village The slopes are farms
Areca nut Betel leaf Black pepper

53
00:02:34,355 --> 00:02:40,005
Jackfruit Broom grass Bamboo This is
the part I had backwards The rain that

54
00:02:40,055 --> 00:02:44,580
soaks the hilltop runs downhill and waters
all of it What drowns my socks up here

55
00:02:44,630 --> 00:02:46,168
feeds the farms down there

56
00:02:46,748 --> 00:02:48,323
Then the broom made sense.

57
00:02:48,436 --> 00:02:52,160
Families here turn broom grass into
brooms and bamboo into baskets,

58
00:02:52,210 --> 00:02:53,048
right at home.

59
00:02:53,161 --> 00:02:55,998
Broom grass is one of the
area's main cash crops.

60
00:02:56,111 --> 00:02:57,423
I tried to make a broom.

61
00:02:57,535 --> 00:02:59,548
It came out shaped like a question mark.

62
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:02,060
And Mawsynram is more than one village.

63
00:03:02,173 --> 00:03:06,173
In the twenty eleven census, it was the
centre of a development block of well over a

64
00:03:06,223 --> 00:03:09,386
hundred villages, with around
fifty-four thousand people.

65
00:03:09,498 --> 00:03:12,836
Its health centre sends a team out
to nearby villages every month.

66
00:03:12,948 --> 00:03:16,660
One of their drivers has said these slopes
wear out his brake pads about every two

67
00:03:16,710 --> 00:03:17,510
weeks.

68
00:03:17,535 --> 00:03:21,110
A road runs from these hills down
toward the border with Bangladesh.

69
00:03:21,223 --> 00:03:23,260
So the hilltop is where things meet.

70
00:03:23,373 --> 00:03:24,960
Crops on the slopes below.

71
00:03:25,073 --> 00:03:26,735
Roads and markets beyond.

72
00:03:26,848 --> 00:03:29,561
People here don't live in the
rain because they love rain.

73
00:03:29,673 --> 00:03:31,511
They live where the rain pays off.

74
00:03:31,623 --> 00:03:34,610
I hung the broom on my
backpack and unpacked one sock.

75
00:03:34,723 --> 00:03:35,523
Just one.

76
00:03:36,106 --> 00:03:38,731
That still left the question
my socks kept asking.

77
00:03:38,843 --> 00:03:40,519
Why does it rain this much here?

78
00:03:40,631 --> 00:03:43,031
I assumed the sea must be right next door.

79
00:03:43,144 --> 00:03:43,944
It isn't.

80
00:03:44,043 --> 00:03:46,356
The Bay of Bengal is far to the south,

81
00:03:46,406 --> 00:03:48,643
beyond the flat plains of Bangladesh.

82
00:03:48,756 --> 00:03:50,356
That flatness is the trick.

83
00:03:50,469 --> 00:03:53,569
In the monsoon, warm wet
air blows north off the bay,

84
00:03:53,619 --> 00:03:55,956
and across the plain
nothing gets in its way.

85
00:03:56,068 --> 00:03:57,493
Then it hits this plateau.

86
00:03:57,606 --> 00:04:00,456
Mawsynram sits about
fourteen hundred metres up,

87
00:04:00,568 --> 00:04:02,518
roughly forty-seven hundred feet.

88
00:04:02,631 --> 00:04:04,393
The air is shoved up the slope.

89
00:04:04,506 --> 00:04:06,906
It cools, and its water falls out.

90
00:04:07,018 --> 00:04:09,144
It works like the lid on a pot of soup.

91
00:04:09,256 --> 00:04:12,744
Steam rises, meets the cool
lid, and drips back down.

92
00:04:12,856 --> 00:04:14,169
These hills are the lid.

93
00:04:14,281 --> 00:04:17,131
I was standing on it,
in June, in a raincoat.

94
00:04:17,712 --> 00:04:19,612
The rain also keeps odd hours.

95
00:04:19,724 --> 00:04:21,987
According to satellite
studies of these hills,

96
00:04:22,037 --> 00:04:25,625
the heaviest rain tends to come
between midnight and early morning.

97
00:04:25,737 --> 00:04:28,074
I hung my laundry out
on a bright afternoon.

98
00:04:28,187 --> 00:04:30,387
By breakfast it was wetter
than when I washed it.

99
00:04:30,499 --> 00:04:35,062
When AFP visited in twenty thirteen, its
reporter wrote that clouds sometimes drift

100
00:04:35,112 --> 00:04:37,575
right into homes and
leave everything damp.

101
00:04:37,687 --> 00:04:40,400
One drifted into my room
and settled on my bed.

102
00:04:40,512 --> 00:04:41,812
I slept on the floor.

103
00:04:41,924 --> 00:04:45,537
A Khasi poet has written about the
local words for different rains.

104
00:04:45,650 --> 00:04:49,187
One of them, lapiwtung, is a rain
that makes your clothes smell.

105
00:04:49,300 --> 00:04:52,062
There are words for a rain
that lasts six nights,

106
00:04:52,112 --> 00:04:53,462
and one that lasts nine.

107
00:04:53,574 --> 00:04:55,262
My suitcase met lapiwtung.

108
00:04:55,375 --> 00:04:58,125
That night, the sock I
had unpacked went back in.

109
00:04:58,237 --> 00:05:01,012
The same report said that
when the rains finally end,

110
00:05:01,062 --> 00:05:02,337
there is no festival.

111
00:05:02,449 --> 00:05:05,187
People just carry their
clothes outside to dry.

112
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:08,675
So I went back in January
and did exactly that.

113
00:05:08,788 --> 00:05:10,462
I carried my sock outside.

114
00:05:10,575 --> 00:05:12,062
It dried in an afternoon.

115
00:05:12,175 --> 00:05:13,413
I nearly framed it.

116
00:05:13,525 --> 00:05:15,488
I unpacked the whole suitcase.

117
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:18,650
I couldn't find official
monthly averages for Mawsynram,

118
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:21,325
so I borrowed the ones
from nearby Cherrapunji.

119
00:05:21,437 --> 00:05:26,237
There, about three quarters of the year's
rain falls between June and September.

120
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:29,850
An average July day brings more
rain than the whole of December.

121
00:05:29,962 --> 00:05:31,925
The dry season really is dry.

122
00:05:32,037 --> 00:05:34,312
And here's where my
bottle of water comes in.

123
00:05:34,425 --> 00:05:38,263
In twenty twenty-four, the local member
of the state assembly said some villages

124
00:05:38,313 --> 00:05:41,012
here run short of drinking
water in the dry months.

125
00:05:41,125 --> 00:05:43,187
The water doesn't stay up on the hilltops,

126
00:05:43,237 --> 00:05:44,725
and streams can run dry.

127
00:05:44,838 --> 00:05:48,613
A headmistress told a reporter in twenty
twenty that people never learned to save

128
00:05:48,663 --> 00:05:51,438
water, because there was
always so much of it.

129
00:05:51,550 --> 00:05:55,587
The same report described rainwater tanks
built by the government and villagers

130
00:05:55,637 --> 00:05:57,712
together, one for each neighbourhood.

131
00:05:57,825 --> 00:06:00,000
The headman said that
because people built them,

132
00:06:00,050 --> 00:06:01,537
they treat them as their own.

133
00:06:01,650 --> 00:06:05,850
I stood beside an empty stream bed, in
one of the rainiest corners of Asia,

134
00:06:05,950 --> 00:06:07,462
drinking water I had bought.

135
00:06:08,044 --> 00:06:10,494
On a clear dry day, from
the edge of the plateau,

136
00:06:10,544 --> 00:06:12,544
you can see the plains of Bangladesh.

137
00:06:12,657 --> 00:06:15,244
I tried to follow one
June raindrop in my head.

138
00:06:15,356 --> 00:06:16,844
It lands on my sock.

139
00:06:16,956 --> 00:06:20,469
It rolls off, joins a
stream, races down the slope,

140
00:06:20,581 --> 00:06:22,044
and drops over the edge.

141
00:06:22,157 --> 00:06:23,194
Lovely form.

142
00:06:23,307 --> 00:06:25,406
The hilltop is a steep tin roof.

143
00:06:25,519 --> 00:06:28,094
Pour a bucket on it, and it gets very wet,

144
00:06:28,219 --> 00:06:30,569
very fast, and keeps almost nothing.

145
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:35,006
In twenty nineteen, Meghalaya's deputy
chief minister said much the same:

146
00:06:35,069 --> 00:06:38,144
the state's rain runs off
quickly, into Bangladesh.

147
00:06:38,256 --> 00:06:40,319
Sometimes the run-off is enormous.

148
00:06:40,431 --> 00:06:42,932
On the seventeenth of
June, twenty twenty-two,

149
00:06:42,982 --> 00:06:46,669
Mawsynram recorded more than a
metre of rain in twenty-four hours.

150
00:06:46,781 --> 00:06:48,569
That's over thirty-nine inches.

151
00:06:48,681 --> 00:06:53,432
Bangladesh's flood forecasters named heavy
rain in Meghalaya and Assam as one cause

152
00:06:53,482 --> 00:06:55,769
of the floods that followed
in the Sylhet region.

153
00:06:55,882 --> 00:06:57,769
Millions of people were affected.

154
00:06:57,882 --> 00:06:59,369
The hill catches the rain.

155
00:06:59,482 --> 00:07:01,832
The plain catches what
the hill can't hold.

156
00:07:02,394 --> 00:07:07,057
Before flying home, I opened my notebook
map and drew one more dotted line,

157
00:07:07,144 --> 00:07:11,369
south and east, down to Khasi and
Jaintia villages on the lower slopes.

158
00:07:11,482 --> 00:07:13,944
That's where most of the
living root bridges grow.

159
00:07:14,056 --> 00:07:17,806
Villagers guide the hanging roots of
a rubber fig tree across a stream,

160
00:07:17,894 --> 00:07:20,457
until they reach the
far bank and take hold.

161
00:07:20,569 --> 00:07:21,532
Then they wait.

162
00:07:21,644 --> 00:07:25,106
The people who start a bridge are
not always the ones who finish it.

163
00:07:25,219 --> 00:07:27,532
Estimates vary for how
long a bridge takes,

164
00:07:27,582 --> 00:07:29,832
from about twenty years
to several decades.

165
00:07:29,944 --> 00:07:33,706
Some bridges are still growing
stronger after more than sixty years.

166
00:07:33,819 --> 00:07:38,144
India nominated them for World Heritage
status in January twenty twenty-six,

167
00:07:38,194 --> 00:07:41,131
and UNESCO is due to look at
them in twenty twenty-seven.

168
00:07:41,244 --> 00:07:45,244
I crossed one very slowly, stepping
only where the roots looked confident.

169
00:07:45,356 --> 00:07:48,169
Nobody plants a bridge in
a place they mean to leave.

170
00:07:48,752 --> 00:07:52,627
Back at my desk, the sock dripped into its
saucer while I read about something else

171
00:07:52,677 --> 00:07:54,627
that ties families to a place.

172
00:07:54,740 --> 00:07:57,340
Khasi families trace their
line through the mother.

173
00:07:57,452 --> 00:08:00,389
By custom, the youngest
daughter, called ka khadduh,

174
00:08:00,439 --> 00:08:03,052
keeps the ancestral house
and the family land.

175
00:08:03,164 --> 00:08:04,552
Keeps, not owns.

176
00:08:04,664 --> 00:08:08,527
It belongs to the whole lineage, and selling
it needs the agreement of her mother's

177
00:08:08,577 --> 00:08:10,490
brothers and her own brothers.

178
00:08:10,602 --> 00:08:12,877
That custom is being debated right now.

179
00:08:12,989 --> 00:08:14,477
But you can see what it does.

180
00:08:14,590 --> 00:08:16,814
It's a bit like a family's one spare key,

181
00:08:16,864 --> 00:08:18,877
held by the person everyone trusts.

182
00:08:18,989 --> 00:08:22,527
A house like that isn't something you sell
because the weather got on your nerves.

183
00:08:23,110 --> 00:08:25,160
The sock still hangs above my desk.

184
00:08:25,273 --> 00:08:27,710
It drips into its saucer every so often,

185
00:08:27,785 --> 00:08:29,410
like a very patient clock.

186
00:08:29,523 --> 00:08:31,372
Mawsynram makes sense to me now.

187
00:08:31,485 --> 00:08:34,772
The rain that soaks the hilltop
runs down to feed the slopes,

188
00:08:34,822 --> 00:08:37,785
and the families up top keep
houses that were kept for them.

189
00:08:37,897 --> 00:08:39,847
I just couldn't keep my things dry.

190
00:08:39,960 --> 00:08:41,423
A cloud took my bed.

191
00:08:41,535 --> 00:08:43,660
The rain seemed to work the night shift.

192
00:08:43,772 --> 00:08:45,847
Lapiwtung got into everything I packed.

193
00:08:45,960 --> 00:08:47,710
None of that is the village's fault.

194
00:08:47,822 --> 00:08:48,622
Nope.

195
00:08:48,635 --> 00:08:50,960
If you can sleep through
rain drumming on the roof,

196
00:08:51,085 --> 00:08:53,998
and you'd like to grow your own
broom, you would do well there.

197
00:08:54,110 --> 00:08:54,973
Bring a knup.

198
00:08:55,085 --> 00:08:56,598
Leave the umbrella at home.

199
00:08:56,710 --> 00:08:58,497
I would be damp, permanently.

200
00:08:58,610 --> 00:08:59,785
The sock agrees.

201
00:08:59,898 --> 00:09:01,735
It dripped again while I was talking.

202
00:09:01,847 --> 00:09:04,397
If you could keep one thing
dry for a whole monsoon,

203
00:09:04,523 --> 00:09:05,372
what would it be?

204
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:08,978
The sock moves to the
shelf, saucer and all.

205
00:09:09,090 --> 00:09:12,128
On its nail, my Kiruna
key leans politely away.

206
00:09:12,240 --> 00:09:14,227
The postcard has started to curl.

207
00:09:14,340 --> 00:09:18,377
Somewhere dry and hot, homes sit below
the ground and look up at the sky.

208
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:20,640
The sock is already leaning toward it.
